  "image_name": "1b9df917741d6e387672a61439416ced.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes tuberculosis infection across scales, from transmission to the immune microenvironment. On the left, inhaled bacteria establish infection in the lung, where they become enclosed in a granuloma. This granuloma is shown as an organized structure with a central caseous core surrounded by infected macrophages, T cells, neutrophils, and an outer fibroblast layer. A related cavitating lesion is also shown, where the granuloma breaks down and releases large numbers of bacteria into the airway, linking lung pathology to bacteria in sputum and onward transmission.\n\nThe right side zooms into a macrophage containing mycobacteria inside a phagosome. One panel depicts antimicrobial mechanisms that appear to restrict bacterial growth, including NADPH oxidase–derived reactive oxygen species, nitric oxide from iNOS, acidification by V-ATPase, and nutrient limitation involving carbon and nitrogen. In contrast, the lower macrophage panel shows a more permissive intracellular state, marked by question marks and an apparently altered vacuole, implying incomplete understanding of how the bacteria survive despite host defenses.\n\nOverall, the figure contrasts containment versus escape: organized immune structures and macrophage killing mechanisms on one hand, and lesion breakdown and bacterial persistence on the other.",
